# nr-office-pdf-glyphs wiring (core): glyph outlines for rendering in the crate `mixdog-pdf` (ported from pdf.js 6.4.299 font_renderer.js, cff_parser.js, type1_parser.js, type1_font.js, standard_fonts.js).

No new crate, no protocol row, no registration and no SERVICE_PENDING entry: nothing in the daemon calls the glyph module yet (the PDF renderer task does).
`ttf-parser` is NOT used and must not be added: pdf.js reads `glyf`, `loca`, `cmap` and `head` with its own small parsers, and the port follows them
(including their errors), so the crate needs no font parsing dependency.

## 1. native/core/crates/mixdog-pdf/src/lib.rs

After the line `pub mod fonts;` add

    pub mod glyph;

(the module is `src/glyph/{mod,truetype,type2,type1,substitute}.rs`, all in this task's tree).

## 2. native/core/crates/mixdog-pdf/Cargo.toml

`[dev-dependencies]` (the library itself gets no new dependency: it does no file I/O, the substitute font files come in through a reader the rendering
configuration supplies), after `flate2 = { workspace = true }` add

    mixdog-fsio = { workspace = true }
    serde_json = { workspace = true }

## 3. native/core/Cargo.lock

In the `[[package]] name = "mixdog-pdf"` entry the dependency list becomes (alphabetical):

    dependencies = [
     "flate2",
     "mixdog-fsio",
     "serde_json",
     "tracing",
    ]

Both are already workspace members / registry crates present in the lock; nothing is downloaded.

## 4. Fixture index

The set `native/core/fixtures/office-glyphs/` (golden.json, fonts/*.otf, MANIFEST.json) is written by `benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-office-glyphs.mjs`.
Refresh the top-level index with

    node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/verify-manifests.mjs --write-index

The set was recorded with pdfjs-dist 6.4.299 on Node 22.20 (no `Float16Array`, and the recorder removes it anyway: paths are Float32 words). Its manifest's
`product` block names the tree the recorder was pointed at (this task's worktree, whose `node_modules/pdfjs-dist` is the owner's); re-record against
`C:\Project\mixdog` with `MIXDOG_BENCH_TREE=<tree> cargo test -p mixdog-pdf --test twin_glyphs -- --ignored` if the identity block must name it.

## 5. What the rendering configuration must do (the PDF renderer task)

The standard fonts are the files of `native/core/crates/mixdog-pdf/assets/standard_fonts/` (fourteen font files and the two licences, copied unchanged
from pdfjs-dist). They are loaded only by the rendering configuration:

    let fonts = mixdog_pdf::glyph::substitute::StandardFonts::new(Box::new(move |file| mixdog_fsio::read(&proof, dir.join(file))));

`dir` is the shipped copy of that folder (pdf.js `standardFontDataUrl`). The office analysis configuration never creates a `StandardFonts`.
Packaging must ship the folder next to the binary or embed it; that is the packaging task's line (no packaging file is touched here).

The sanitised font program pdf.js hands its renderer (TrueType repair, OpenType wrapping of CFF and of converted Type 1, the `cmap` it builds) is the
font-object task's: `FontRenderer::create(sfnt_bytes, font_matrix)` takes its result, and `FontRenderer::from_type2(Type1Font::parse(..)?.to_type2(), cmap,
font_matrix)` takes a Type 1 program with the cmap the font object built for it.
